# t01_avsync.ledc -- AUDIO-vs-LED SYNC, multi-rate.  ~56 s
# NOT therapeutic.  Feature family: A/V alignment, rate changes.
#
# WHY MULTI-RATE: a real latency holds a constant TIME offset across rates while
# an envelope-shape artifact holds a constant PHASE.  Running 4/8/16 Hz in one
# session is what separates them (av_sync_offset_measured_2026-10-12.md
# "Method": at 2/4/8 Hz the constant-latency model fit to 0.03 ms while
# constant-phase needed 39.9 deg of spread).  The LED square gate and the audio
# square gate (env 0) are commanded at the SAME rate, duty and phase, so the
# offset between their envelopes IS the sync error.
#
# Expect a constant -2.5 .. -3.5 ms (audio EARLY) in EVERY block, including
# across the rate change, after V-S1..V-S4.
#
# WHY 4 / 8 / 16 Hz AND NOT 3 / 6 / 12: with the tick primer below the flicker
# ISR runs at 10 kHz, and 4, 8 and 16 Hz all divide 10000 exactly (2500, 1250
# and 625 ticks), so LED rate quantisation contributes exactly zero to the
# numbers here.  3, 6 and 12 Hz do not (833.33 ticks at 12 Hz), which would add
# an up-to-800 ppm period error on top of the thing being measured.
#
# RIG: ch1/ch2 = device headphone jack L/R via a TRS splitter into Hi-Z inputs.
#      ch3 = photocell on one eye, loaded with 220R-1k.  Use a LINE/instrument
#      input, never a microphone: a room adds reverb and ~3 ms per metre.
#   python3 tools/avmeasure/avsync.py rec.wav --light 3 --audio 1 \
#       --carrier 400 --marker-hz 3000 \
#       --rates 4:2:15,8:17:25,16:27:45,8:47:55
#   python3 tools/avmeasure/ratechange.py rec.wav --light 3 --audio 1 \
#       --f-old 16 --f-new 8 --entry 46.03 --win 6 --marker-hz 3000
#
#
#
# CAPTURE-INTEGRITY CARRIER: this file needs no extra tone.  ch1's 400 Hz sine
# runs unbroken from t=1 s to t=56 s and its gate is only 10 % deep (mod_depth is
# hardcoded 0.1), so the carrier never actually shuts:
#   python3 tools/avmeasure/captureintegrity.py rec.wav --carrier 400 --audio-ch 1
# Run it BEFORE believing any per-cycle number.  A recorder can lose a block of
# frames mid-capture and still write a file of exactly the right length -- five
# such steps were found in one 665 s capture, and one was written up as a
# firmware defect because BOTH optical sensors agreed on it.  They agreed because
# the whole recording stepped.  Differential measurements (the A/V offset this
# file exists to make, inter-eye offsets, harmonic ratios) are immune, because
# every input channel steps together; ABSOLUTE per-cycle timing is not.
# No extra tone is added here deliberately: an ungated 1900 Hz carrier would add
# a DC pedestal to avsync's |x| envelope and cut the gate contrast from ~7 % to
# ~4.5 %, roughly doubling the per-edge scatter.
# CAPTURE-CHANNEL MAP used throughout sessions/test (see README.md):
#   capture ch1,ch2 = device headphone jack L,R   capture ch3 = photocell on
#   lane B's eye (LED3+LED4, mask 12)             capture ch4 = photocell on
#   lane A's eye (LED1+LED2, mask 3).
# --------------------------------------------------------------- sync marker
# 600 ms of full-brightness white on every channel plus a 3 kHz tone.  Both
# edges are sharp, which is the whole point: without a marker the analyzer can
# only cross-correlate.  Copied verbatim from sessions/library/90_measure_sync.
0     40 50 100 255 255 255 15
A 0   3000 0 70 0 1
600   0 50 0 0 0 0 15
A 600 0 0 0 0 1

# ---- ISR TICK PRIMER, and it is NOT decoration.
# The flicker gptimer is DELETED whenever no channel is left running, and the
# next activation re-derives its tick from the 1000 Hz FLOOR.  The marker's
# `freq 0` stop at 600 ms stops every channel, so the 40 Hz marker does NOT pin
# the tick -- without this row a first block at 4 Hz would set the tick to
# clamp(4*250,1000,10000) = 1000 Hz and, because an update on an already-running
# channel never calls s_ensure_timer_and_task, every LATER block would stay on
# that 1 ms grid too.
# This row keeps ONE channel alive at 40 Hz for the whole file, which pins the
# tick at 10000 Hz = a 100 us grid for every block below.  mask 16 = LED5, which
# neither sensor can see (-78 / -74 dBFS, the noise floor), and brightness is 0
# regardless, so it cannot contaminate any measurement on any unit.
# It does raise LEDC_MASK_HIGH_BITS (info) in the linter, by design.
1000 40 50 0 0 0 0 16 0 0

# ---- B1  1..16 s : 4 Hz, 60 cycles.  mask 15 = all zones in phase, so a single
#      sensor gets the maximum optical signal and nothing cancels.
#      RGB 255 + bright 100 = effective drive 255, the linear region.
1000   4 50 100 255 255 255 15 0 0
A 1000 400 0 70 4 1 0 0 50 0 0

# ---- B2  16..26 s : 8 Hz, 80 cycles
16000   8 50 100 255 255 255 15 0 0
A 16000 400 0 70 8 1 0 0 50 0 0

# ---- B3  26..46.03 s : 16 Hz, 320 cycles.  The second half of this block is
#      the pre-change reference for B4.
26000   16 50 100 255 255 255 15 0 0
A 26000 400 0 70 16 1 0 0 50 0 0

# ---- B4  46.03..56 s : RATE CHANGE 16 -> 8 Hz at a DELIBERATELY UNALIGNED
#      instant.  20030 ms after the 16 Hz entry is 320.48 old periods, so the
#      next old-rate cycle boundary does NOT coincide with the entry.  Before
#      V-S4 that put the whole post-change block up to +/-31 ms out (half an old
#      period); it must now read the same -2.5..-3.5 ms as B2.
#      avsync.py only knows the offset MODULO the period, so grade the landing
#      INSTANT with ratechange.py and the residual offset with avsync.py.
46030   8 50 100 255 255 255 15 0 0
A 46030 400 0 70 8 1 0 0 50 0 0

# ---- end: freq 0 stops flicker, vol 0 silences.  Both masks are stopped --
#      a channel left flickering past the last entry is cancelled by the
#      end-of-timeline handler without ever having been graded.
56000 0 50 0 0 0 0 15
56000 0 50 0 0 0 0 16
A 56000 400 0 0 0 1 0 0 50 0 0
